---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What are collocations in IELTS writing?",
    answer:
      "Collocations are word combinations that sound natural together, such as 'take action' or 'play a role'. They help your writing sound more fluent and natural.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do collocations improve IELTS scores automatically?",
    answer:
      "No. They help only when they match the meaning and sound natural in the sentence. Forced collocations often make writing worse, not better.",
  },
  {
    question: "How should I study collocations for IELTS?",
    answer:
      "Study them by topic and function, then use each one in your own sentences. It is better to control a smaller set well than memorize a long list poorly.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "Collocations for IELTS Writing", path: "/blog/collocations-ielts/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="Collocations for IELTS Writing | Use Them Naturally, Not For Show"
  description="A practical IELTS collocations guide with useful word combinations, safer usage advice, and a better way to study them."
  type="article"
  keywords="IELTS collocations, collocations for IELTS writing, IELTS vocabulary chunks"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">IELTS vocabulary guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">Collocations for IELTS Writing</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Collocations can make IELTS writing sound more natural, but only when they fit the
        meaning of the sentence. Good collocations are not decoration. They are useful chunks
        of language that native speakers expect to hear together.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is better to use ten collocations accurately than fifty collocations badly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What strong collocation use looks like</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The collocation fits the topic and meaning.</li>
          <li>It sounds natural inside the sentence.</li>
          <li>You can control the grammar around it.</li>
          <li>You do not repeat the same chunk too often.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What weak collocation use looks like</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The phrase is copied but the meaning is unclear.</li>
          <li>The sentence around it is unnatural.</li>
          <li>The collocation is too advanced for the idea.</li>
          <li>The same phrase appears in every paragraph.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>High-value collocations by function</h2>
      <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
        <div class="guide-example">
          <p><strong>Presenting arguments</strong></p>
          <p>play a role</p>
          <p>take action</p>
          <p>have an impact on</p>
          <p>take into account</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-example">
          <p><strong>Cause and effect</strong></p>
          <p>lead to</p>
          <p>result in</p>
          <p>give rise to</p>
          <p>as a consequence</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-example">
          <p><strong>Problems and solutions</strong></p>
          <p>address the issue</p>
          <p>tackle the problem</p>
          <p>alleviate pressure</p>
          <p>implement a solution</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-example">
          <p><strong>Education and society</strong></p>
          <p>acquire skills</p>
          <p>broaden horizons</p>
          <p>social inequality</p>
          <p>public health</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>How to study them better</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Learn the meaning first</h3>
            <p>Do not memorize a phrase until you know exactly what it means.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Write your own sentence</h3>
            <p>Use the collocation in a sentence about a familiar IELTS topic.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Group by function</h3>
            <p>Study collocations for argument, cause and effect, and solutions separately.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Reuse them naturally</h3>
            <p>Bring them into essays only when they fit the idea without forcing them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>The biggest collocation mistake</h2>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          The biggest mistake is choosing a phrase because it sounds impressive. If the
          collocation does not fit the sentence naturally, simple language is safer and often
          better.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/lessons/vocabulary">
          <strong>Browse vocabulary lessons</strong>
          <span>Build more topic vocabulary and useful language chunks.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/ielts/writing">
          <strong>Go to the IELTS writing hub</strong>
          <span>See where vocabulary fits into structure, support, and task response.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/essay-correction">
          <strong>Get writing feedback</strong>
          <span>See whether the vocabulary in your own essay sounds natural.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
